    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Cylinder was a foundation deposit in the Babylonian tradition, made after the Persian conquest of 539 BCE, and its text reviles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> while presenting Cyrus as the king chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>.</li><li>Biblical scholars long read it as confirming Cyrus's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, but it names only Mesopotamian sanctuaries and does not mention Jews, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> or Judea.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> promoted it as the first charter of human rights; most historians regard that reading as anachronistic and see an ordinary royal building inscription.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A widely circulated translation that credits the Cylinder with abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and guaranteeing a minimum wage and asylum is false: it makes claims that are not on the real cylinder and names the Zoroastrian god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a> instead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

